    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Done. The build succeeds and the test passes (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`), and the audit log confirms the new behaviour: an owner registered on 2026-10-01 now gets `membershipNumber=NSW-16E6609E-M27` (fiscal year FY27, since the fiscal year starting 1 July 2026 is named for its ending calendar year 2027).\n\n## What I changed\n\n**`model/Owner.java`**\n- Added a private `fiscalYearNumber(LocalDate)` helper: dates in July\u2013December belong to the next calendar year's fiscal year (named for the year it ends), Jan\u2013June to the current one.\n- Added public `fiscalYear(LocalDate)` returning `FY<YY>` (two-digit fiscal year), null-safe.\n- `membershipNumber(...)` now derives its `M<YY>` segment from the fiscal year rather than the raw calendar year \u2014 this is the behaviour change to the identifier.\n- `membershipPoints(...)` tenure now counts **elapsed fiscal years** (`fiscalYearNumber(now) - fiscalYearNumber(registrationDate) > 1`) instead of a 365-day window, and I dropped the now-unused `ChronoUnit` import and updated the Javadoc.\n\n**`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`**\n- Added a `fiscalYear` mapping + `formatFiscalYear(owner)` deriving it from the (already business-day-adjusted) registration date.\n\n**`resources/openapi.yml`**\n- Added the read-only `fiscalYear` property to the owner response schema (so the generated `OwnerDto` carries the field) and updated the `membershipNumber`/`membershipPoints` descriptions to match.\n\nBecause the registration date is rolled to a business day *before* any derivation in `ClinicServiceImpl.save`, all three values (fiscalYear, membershipNumber, tenure) are automatically computed from the business-day-adjusted date, as the spec requires.\n\nOne interpretation decision worth flagging: I used the Australian convention where the fiscal year is **named for the calendar year in which it ends** (1 July 2026 \u2013 30 June 2027 \u2192 `FY27`). The test only asserts the `FY\\d{2}` shape, so if you intended the start-year convention (`FY26`) instead, it's a one-line change in `fiscalYearNumber`.",
